So please do come and chat to the people down there

25
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.360
if you're interested to learn more about us.

26
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.080
So AI native engineering works.

27
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.440
And I know this because I've seen the numbers and on greenfield

28
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.400
code bases, the benefits of using AI engineering are completely undeniable.

29
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.000
It's a perfect place, a match made in heaven.

30
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.880
We can do things right from the start, being to design the product

31
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.440
and the architecture decisions.

32
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:36.520
They're all kept together in one spec and we can use this as a basis for our build.

33
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.360
We define what system should do.

34
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.720
We get our agents to handle the implementation.

35
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.720
And this means that product managers can have more input

36
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:44.800
in the development process.

37
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.800
We get to prototypes earlier, we get UX feedback quicker,

38
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.000
development teams can move faster.

39
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.680
We start building and testing and observable

40
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.680
much, much earlier in the process.

41
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.720
And a lot of the talks I've seen at this conference, and to be

42
00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.600
fair, are other conferences as well tend to focus on this.

43
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.360
And that's also where a lot of the metrics that get reported in the press seem

44
00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.960
to come from these type of bills.

45
00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.160
And yeah, we've seen the same form and we're saying the same things as well.

46
00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.600
So yeah, 80% faster kickoff.

47
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.120
We are able to go through our product and design process

48
00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.560
in a much more slick, rapid and realistic way.

49
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.280
We've reduced our sprint zero times because we're getting our artifacts

50
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:31.160
from our discovery process, which then is bootstrapping our sprint zero.

51
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.800
So the timelines for that have shrunk with 50% faster to MVP.

52
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.720
We've seen four times development philosophy and all of this.

53
00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.200
That means that there is much more time to focus in on some of the things

54
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.960
that can get lost when you're trying to build out to that MVP very quickly.

55
00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.720
So the nonfunctional considerations are brought in from day one,

56
00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.880
things like accessibility, security and performance.

57
00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.760
And as Hannah Foxwell said yesterday, the velocity problem is

58
00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.760
not really the development problem anymore.

59
00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.520
It's we can all move a lot faster, except not all of us can,

60
00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.960
because in the brownfield the reality is very different.

61
00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.200
Mature code bases account for an estimated 60

62
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:18.080
to 70% of enterprise software, and they are very successful.

63
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.240
They're hugely successful.

64
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.160
That's why they've been around for five, ten, 15 years.

65
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.440
But with that comes accumulated technical debt,

66
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.360
tribal knowledge, as if you ever worked on a code base

67
00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.200
where something was developed by somebody who's long left the organization.

68
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.120
And really the only the only documentation was in their head,

69
00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.480
and they meant to write it down before they left.

70
00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:39.920
Dependency. Right.

71
00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.200
You may be falling behind

72
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.760
with a couple of dependencies and suddenly upgrading to the latest version

73
00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.760
of nodes going to take two months rather than two days.

74
00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.600
Fear driven development places in the code base where people just don't want to go.

75
00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.360
They don't want to touch it because it's so fragile.

76
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:55.880
Test property.

77
00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:01.480
And this may be an unpopular thing to say, but tests didn't always get written.

78
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.360
When you're under a lot of pressure.

79
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.560
Tests are some of those things that can get left behind, or

80
00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.920
they become so brittle that they they're meaningless.

81
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.960
So integration tests and user tests with Cypress,

82
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.320
we've we've seen them just die off and then people ignore them.

83
00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.680
And then there's pointless having them.

84
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.920
They're interdependent systems that are tightly coupled,

85
00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.880
invisible contracts that you just can't see.

86
00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.760
And that results in high maintenance costs.

87
00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.320
Developers send more time deciphering the code and fixing bugs

88
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.080
and resolving issues than implementing new features and improvements.

89
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.800
Small updates become really difficult, change becomes hard.

90
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.560
Future development starts to crawl.

91
00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.040
Anyone who stuck in this space as well becomes very demotivated.

92
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.080
And it's nobody's fault because every decision on this code

93
00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.960
base was made with the best possible intentions.

94
00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.280
Nobody wants the code to get like this,

95
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.280
and it's sometimes referred to as the ball of mud pattern.

96
00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.360
I don't think of brownfield code as a ball of mud.

97
00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.000
I think of them much more like a city.

98
00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:03.960
A city is

99
00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.040
very successful, and it's something that's evolved over time.

100
00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.720
And brownfield systems tend to work like this.

101
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.200
They work incredibly well.

102
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.560
They have huge numbers of customers working on them,

103
00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.600
and that is why they've been around for five, ten, 15 years

104
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.720
whilst maintaining complex user demands, whilst and all the time working

105
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.480
through problems like performance, security and scaling that come with that.

106
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.040
And like the city, they've evolved as a response to the needs

107
00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:35.320
and there are areas in that functioning city.

108
00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:36.480
So this is actually London.

109
00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.160
I took this last weekend from one of the top of the skyscrapers.

110
00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:40.280
I'm very pleased with this picture.

111
00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.040
It's lovely, but you can see on this you've got paths through the city

112
00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.360
which are very, very clear.

113
00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:45.880
These are

114
00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.480
these are the well-trodden paths, the ones that we all know where it's going.

115
00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.600
And then there are less well maintained spaces, dead ends

116
00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:56.760
and the places that you really wouldn't want to get caught in alone late at night.

117
00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.520
So this is like your code base, and you want to evolve it into something

118
00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.000
that is easier to build on, that's more understood,

119
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.520
and you get that high level view so you can start working through.

120
00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.280
So I want to talk to you about how to evolve your

121
00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.320
your architectures on brownfield code basis.

122
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:18.200
And I've got three different methodologies that you can have

123
00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.320
that you can start thinking about.

124
00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.680
If you're wanting to move your brownfield code base on to its next steps.

125
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.640
First off is the pseudo greenfield development,

126
00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.240
and I think this one, I've seen this happen quite a lot.

127
00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.120
It's really great when your legacy code base

128
00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.280
is really coupled or fragile and developing inside it has become

129
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.680
very slow velocity, starting to crawl and effectively.

130
00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.920
What you do is you branch out and say, write this new feature,

131
00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:46.680
I'm going to develop it, and I'm going to treat it like a greenfield code base.

132
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:52.320
Now, when you do this, you tend to see a really fast iteration at the beginning.

133
00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:56.840
You can move very, very quickly, but then you get to the point

134
00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:01.480
where you're starting to merge the branch back in and things start to slow down.

135
00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:05.280
It's it's really, really fantastic.

136
00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.760
And people are doing this.

137
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:12.240
The developers who are working on it start to become kind of tourists.

138
00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.720
They never learn about the underlying problems of the code base.

139
00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.400
You can end up with duplication of shared concerns.

140
00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.680
So things like authentication, logging, error handling, you may end up on your

141
00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.920
your pseudo greenfield branch, duplicating things that the legacy system

142
00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:29.240
has already solved, which means that you end up with twice

143
00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.840
the amount of complexity because you've got diverging approaches.

144
00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.840
The longer your greenfield branch runs in isolation,

145
00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:41.120
the more the branch diverges, the more expensive it is to do

146
00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.240
re-integration without discipline as well.

147
00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:45.360
This temporary isolation

148
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.480
strategy can become a permanent fork, which is another problem,

149
00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.760
and you don't really get to test

150
00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:56.400
how the code is integrating well together until you get to

151
00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.600
that last .0.4 here, merging it back into the code base.

152
00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:04.320
And that means that you can end up with a huge amount of bugs

153
00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:08.080
and test and refactoring as a result of that reintegration.

154
00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:11.360
But it is one development pattern

155
00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:16.160
and it can lead to the next one, which is the strangler fig pattern.

156
00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.560
So what is a strangler fig?

157
00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.680
This is always worth talking about. Strangler.

158
00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:24.800
The strangler pattern was defined by Martin Fowler,

159
00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:28.200
I think back in the 2000, and the idea

160
00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:31.360
is that it's based on a strangler fig vine.

161
00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.480
So the vine drops the seed and it's part of a tree, and it drops the seed

162
00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.960
and it sends down a root opposite to what this diagram is doing.

163
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.080
And then it builds up self around it, slowly strangling out the nutrients

164
00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:48.080
from the tree until it remains and the old tree itself is removed.

165
00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.640
So like that, the strangle fed pattern takes away the responsibility

166
00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.640
from the old area of code, one slice at a time.

167
00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.320
And it's really great when you need to do

168
00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:01.680
an entire system replacement alongside the original with no downtime.

169
00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:03.680
This

170
00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:08.600
is a pattern that's been used by Uber, Netflix, and BBC when they were evolving

171
00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:14.080
to move to microservices, and it's great because it doesn't require downtimes.

172
00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.400
If you've got a legacy system that's poorly understood,

173
00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.120
you can intercept at the edge.

174
00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.400
And I've used it on systems before where you've had so much dependency

175
00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:28.120
that you just can't build the code on your machine anymore,

176
00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:31.840
and it's the only way that you can then take the responsibility

177
00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:34.240
away from something which may still be running in production,

178
00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:38.240
but you actually need to evolve massive advantages.

179
00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:42.440
Yeah, the code that you're writing is effectively greenfield,

180
00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:45.800
but it's based on the legacy functionality.

181
00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:49.040
Sorry.

182
00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:50.960
And replicating.

183
00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:54.000
You can leave behind the bad patterns, which is always good as well.

184
00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:57.080
There's no need to touch the legacy code bases before

185
00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:00.960
you create a facade which makes a boundary.

186
00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.080
So you're keeping the old system frozen.

187
00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:04.360
You're routing.

188
00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:06.880
The routing layer provides a natural place for feature flags.

189
00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:08.400
You can do a B testing, you can do

190
00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:12.360
canary testing, you can do traffic rollback, and progress is visible.

191
00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:13.360
So you can start seeing

192
00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:16.560
that new traffic hitting the old system so you can test it very well.

193
00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:20.160
The disadvantages with this system is that it requires

194
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.400
effectively setting up and running to systems.

195
00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.280
And it's not a trivial cost in the boundary, especially for like

196
00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:31.440
you've got to maintain two infrastructures effectively.

197
00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.640
It does take some time to

198
00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:39.960
work out where the points of abstraction should be, where you can

199
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:44.280
break these things apart, and it requires a commitment to completion.

200
00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:45.560
There's an awful lot of half

201
00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:49.200
finished strangler patterns out there where you've got some of the

202
00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:52.680
some of the process has been started, some of the responsibility

203
00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:53.840
has been taken away.

204
00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:57.360
And then the impetus for continuing with it has been lost, which means that you're

205
00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:00.960
then left with a double maintenance headache, which can be

206
00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.720
caused a lot more stress and a lot more complexity for the teams.

207
00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:10.240
So a third option is the brands by extraction.

208
00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:12.440
So with branch by abstraction,

209
00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:16.800
you are working from inside the code base rather than around it.

210
00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:21.080
You put an interface within the code and you can then branch out

211
00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:22.360
to a new implementation.

212
00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:24.040
So something behind a feature flag,

213
00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:27.160
so that you then can route the traffic through to your new feature flag.

214
00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.080
It's great when you've got internal improvements

215
00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:34.440
and the codes that need to remove from the area, so you can get rid of

216
00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:39.280
bad patterns or rot that may have crept into your code base.

217
00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.480
When you need to remove a deeply embedded component, this is one way to do it.

218
00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:47.080
It keeps trunk releasable at all times.

219
00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:50.560
The abstraction layer makes the seam explicit,

220
00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.480
and you can easier to reason about what's old versus new.

221
00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:57.560
Both implementations are active at once, so you can compare behavior on production.

222
00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:00.480
So you just switch on your feature flag, check how it's working.

223
00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:02.160
That means you're testing as you're going,

224
00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.760
and that means that you're getting that constant feedback.

225
00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:08.720
And it actually naturally surfaces a lot of hidden dependencies and coupling

226
00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:12.320
that maybe other factors might miss the abstraction.

227
00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:16.280
The disadvantages is that it is another piece of code

228
00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:19.080
that you have to write and maintain and eventually delete.

229
00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:23.480
There's certain code bases which are littered with these abstractions

230
00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:26.480
that haven't been completely deleted, which again

231
00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:29.320
adds to the cognitive complexity of the code base.

232
00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:33.480
And if teams kind of lose motivation and leave these things

233
00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:37.880
half finished, if you don't have a driver to complete it,

234
00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:41.440
it's also quite tedious to find these things

235
00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:45.520
in a large ground field code base, which is where AI can come in to help you.

236
00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:50.880
And the toggle and the routing logic does add cognitive load overhead.

237
00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.720
Like I've mentioned, it is much more like a

238
00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:56.600
if you imagine a body right, and you had to replace a kidney,

239
00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:59.720
if you go in there and you slice it and you remove the kidney and replace

240
00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:02.880
the kidney, it's much more like that sort of methodology.

241
00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:04.800
What horrible metaphor.

242
00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:07.320
I'm sorry,

243
00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:10.320
but you all know what I mean, don't you?

244
00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:11.400
Right.

245
00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:14.840
So how do we use AI to apply these methodologies?

246
00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.840
